<p>A <strong>delayed insurance claim in Port Arthur</strong> can be more exhausting than a denial. There is no clear answer to respond to, only unreturned calls, new adjusters, repeated document requests and a roof tarp that is wearing out. Some delay is normal after a major hurricane, when an insurer may be handling an enormous number of claims along the upper Texas coast at once. But Texas law does set timelines for claim handling, and a claim that has drifted well past them deserves attention. We help Port Arthur and Jefferson County property owners understand where their claim stands, organize the record and push the file toward a decision.</p>

<h2>Texas Prompt Payment Timelines</h2>
<p>The Prompt Payment of Claims provisions in <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, Subchapter B</a> set general deadlines for most insurers:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>General deadline</th><th>Section</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin the investigation and request needed information</td><td>15 days after the insurer receives notice of the claim</td><td>§542.055</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim in writing</td><td>15 business days after receiving all items the insurer requested</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>If more time is needed, notify the policyholder</td><td>Within the same 15 business days; decision then due within 45 more days</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay an accepted claim</td><td>5 business days after notifying the policyholder of acceptance</td><td>§542.057</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather-related catastrophe declared by the commissioner</td><td>Deadlines extended by an additional 15 days</td><td>§542.059</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A few important qualifications. The 15-business-day clock for a decision starts after the insurer receives everything it reasonably requested, which is why the timing of document requests matters. Surplus lines insurers are treated differently. TWIA claims fall under their own rules in Chapter 2210; our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page and our <a href="/twia-windstorm-claim-guide-port-arthur/">TWIA claim guide</a> explain those. If you believe the statute has been violated, the remedies are a legal question for a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>Why Claims Stall After Gulf Coast Storms</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Catastrophe volume.</strong> After hurricanes such as Laura or Harvey, insurers and TWIA bring in temporary adjusters to handle claims across Southeast Texas and Southwest Louisiana.</li>
<li><strong>Adjuster turnover.</strong> Files are reassigned as catastrophe adjusters rotate out, and new adjusters may start over.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple policies.</strong> A homeowners carrier may wait on the TWIA or flood decision before acting, and vice versa.</li>
<li><strong>Open-ended requests.</strong> Requests for “additional documentation” without saying what is needed keep the decision clock from starting.</li>
<li><strong>Engineer and expert reports.</strong> Outside reports can add weeks, and the results are not always shared promptly.</li>
<li><strong>Disagreement in silence.</strong> Sometimes a claim is not delayed so much as stuck, because the adjuster disagrees with the scope but has not said so in writing.</li>
</ul>

