<h2>How to Build a Record That Moves a Claim</h2>
<p>The single most effective thing a policyholder can do about delay is document it. A clear timeline turns “they’re dragging their feet” into specific dates that can be shown to the insurer, TDI or an attorney.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Log every contact.</strong> Date, time, name, what was said and what was promised.</li>
<li><strong>Move important conversations to writing.</strong> After a phone call, send a short email confirming what was discussed.</li>
<li><strong>Ask what is missing.</strong> Request a written list of every item the insurer still needs to make a decision.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm delivery.</strong> Send documents in a way that shows the date received, and keep copies.</li>
<li><strong>Request status in writing.</strong> If a decision deadline has passed, ask in writing for the claim’s status and the reason for the delay.</li>
<li><strong>Keep protecting the property.</strong> Replace worn tarps, keep drying equipment running and photograph conditions over time.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> and the article on <a href="/how-to-document-storm-damage-port-arthur/">documenting storm damage in Port Arthur</a> cover the evidence side.</p>

<h2>Delays on Flood and Multi-Policy Claims</h2>
<p>Flood claims under the National Flood Insurance Program follow federal rules rather than the Texas Prompt Payment statute, including their own proof-of-loss requirements and deadlines. Check <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">floodsmart.gov</a> and your flood policy for the current requirements, and do not let a slow homeowners or TWIA claim cause you to miss a flood deadline. When several insurers are involved, it can help to keep a separate log for each claim, with its own claim number, adjuster, requests and deadlines. A single shared timeline is useful too, because each insurer’s decision may depend on what the others have found.</p>

<h2>What Reasonable Delay Looks Like</h2>
<p>Not every delay is improper. After a declared catastrophe, deadlines are extended, and complex losses, such as large commercial buildings or homes needing an engineer’s report, can legitimately take longer. The warning signs are different: no written acknowledgment, no clear list of what the insurer still needs, deadlines that pass without a request for more time, or months with no meaningful activity on the file.</p>

<h2>When a Delay Becomes Something Else</h2>
<p>Sometimes a long delay ends with a low offer or a partial denial. If that happens, the next step depends on what the insurer decided. An inadequate payment may call for an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claim review</a> and supplement; a disagreement over the amount may lead to <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a>; a coverage denial may require legal advice. Keep in mind that many Texas policies set a contractual deadline to file suit, often two years and one day, and that weather-related claim suits require written pre-suit notice under Chapter 542A. A slow claim does not pause those clocks, so talk with a Texas attorney if time is getting short.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>When we take on a delayed claim, we start by reconstructing the timeline: when the claim was reported, what the insurer requested, what was provided and when. We fill documentation gaps, prepare a complete estimate if one does not exist and communicate with the adjuster in writing so the file has a clear record. As a Port Arthur Public Adjuster, we cannot force a decision or guarantee an outcome, but a well-organized claim with no open requests gives the insurer little reason to wait.</p>
<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If your claim has stalled, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]].</p>
